WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.160
Removable media comes in several common forms.

00:00:03.380 --> 00:00:08.660
USB flash drives are small, inexpensive, and frequently used for quick transfers.

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:15.920
External hard drives and external SSDs can hold large data sets, backups, virtual machines, and archives.

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:22.700
SD and microSD cards may appear in cameras, drones, phones, embedded devices, and adapters.

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:31.800
Optical media is less common than it once was, but CDs, DVDs, or Blu-ray discs may still appear in older environments or specialized workflows.

00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:39.780
Mobile devices can also appear as removable storage or as media transfer devices depending on operating system, configuration, and connection mode.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:46.960
This matters because the investigator may see files exposed through a storage-like interface even when the underlying device is more complex.

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:53.420
The fact that a phone or camera exposes files does not mean the examiner is seeing every relevant artifact on that device.

00:00:53.640 --> 00:01:05.960
File systems commonly found on removable media include FAT variants, exFAT, NTFS, APFS, HFS Plus, ext variants, and other formats depending on platform and use.

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:13.220
Each file system has its own metadata behavior, timestamp precision, deletion behavior, and compatibility tradeoffs.

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:18.080
A timestamp or deleted-file finding should always be interpreted with the file system in mind.

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:20.060
Device identity can be messy.

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:22.040
Volume labels can be changed.

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:28.020
Serial numbers may be missing, duplicated, generic, or reported differently by hosts and tools.

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:35.600
Device identifiers may come from the USB controller, storage device, operating system, volume metadata, or host logs.

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:39.580
Some identifiers describe the enclosure rather than the drive inside it.

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:44.040
Others may change when a drive is reformatted or connected through a different adapter.

00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:56.900
A practical investigation should record the physical device, label text, manufacturer markings, capacity, connector type, observed serial numbers, volume names, file system, and acquisition identifiers.

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:05.120
Identity evidence is strongest when multiple independent details line up across the media, host artifacts, and chain-of-custody documentation.
